  "notes": "1017-orbit trap written from a keyboard rather than assembled from a loop, and the research's own distinguishing claim is the one this model spends its budget on: \"the melody is a running arpeggio rather than a motif\". `melody.densityPerBar` [7,12] is the highest on this bench by a wide margin, `intervals.third` is raised to 0.4 because an arpeggio moves in thirds rather than steps, and `countermelody.styles` leads with `arp` to match. The second claim is real chord motion \"rather than drones\", so the i–VI–III–VII and i–iv–V families the research names carry the weight and `harmonicRhythm` keeps no `vamp`. The drums are the busy end: the highest `kick.densityPerBar` here at [4,7] with `syncopation` 0.68, and dense 32nd fill work carried by `hihat.fill` rather than by the roll vocabulary. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium — a gear feature plus a discography, not a per-record production account. The inferred parameters are the note-density bounds (the research says only \"higher note density than the ATL norm\") and the `kick` numbers. ⚠ The low-weight i–VII–VI family is INFERRED; the research names only the two above.",
